  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day and into tonight, our primary focus shifts\nto an advancing mass of strong to severe thunderstorms \ncurrently organizing in the Red River Valley. This complex has \nmoved up in time for impacts to the Northland this evening \ncompared to earlier forecasts and is now timed to enter our \nnorth-central Minnesota zones around 5 PM this evening. The \nstorms will ride a sharp instability gradient, driving \nsoutheastward primarily south of the Iron Range. The main \nhazards with this line will be damaging wind gusts and large \nhail up to the size of ping-pong balls, with the potential for \nwind-driven hail. As the system tracks eastward, the severe \nthreat is expected to drop off rapidly an hour or two after \nsunset as it approaches the western Iron Range and the Twin \nPorts region, with the highest damaging wind threat remaining \nfurther south along the Interstate 35 corridor. Expect this \nactivity to clear the Minnesota/Wisconsin stateline late this \nevening into the early overnight hours.\n\nIn the wake of the evening convection, lingering low-level\nmoisture and cooling temperatures could produce dense fog \ntonight. Of particular note is a bank of very low stratus over\nthe North Shore terrain ridge, which could significantly reduce\nvisibilities for the Friday morning commute. Conditions will \ngradually improve by late Friday morning as ceilings lift and \nskies slowly clear out into the midday hours. Along the shores \nof western Lake Superior, strong and persistent northeast winds \nwill continue to generate dangerous rip currents, keeping \nswimming conditions hazardous. Another Beach Hazards Statement\nmay again by needed for Friday after the current one today\nexpires late this evening.\n\nHeading into the weekend, temperatures will gradually warm back\ninto the upper-70s to low-80s under the influence of transient \nupper-level ridging. The break in active weather will be \nrelatively short- lived, however, as the next surge of \ninstability builds into the region by Sunday. We will be \nmonitoring the potential for strong thunderstorms Sunday \nafternoon through overnight. While severe weather is not \ncurrently forecast for Sunday late PM, the environment will be \ncharacterized by an incredibly moist airmass, raising concerns \nfor heavy rainfall that then lasts in Monday.\n\nThis moist, unsettled pattern will linger into Labor Day. \nAreawide rainfall chances peak on Monday with a 60 to 80 percent\nprobability of precipitation as a low-pressure system lifts from\nthe northern U.S. Plains into southern Manitoba. The \nprecipitation is forecast to slowly clear out of the Northland \nby Tuesday morning. Looking further ahead, as the precipitation \ndeparts and broad troughing re-establishes itself, temperatures\nare expected to slowly cool to near normal through the latter \nhalf of next week.",
